// THE WALK HANDS THE ELEMENT AND THE INDEX, EACH ITS OWN VALUE.
//
// A fixed array satisfies Range intrinsically (std/core/range.zen), so
// `xs.loop((h, i, v) ..)` lowers through lower_array_walk — a plain C for
// whose pass value is read STRAIGHT out of the member (`zg_elems[i]`),
// not called. Two bindings arrive at the body and they are different
// things: the counter and the element. Swap them and the program still
// compiles — the weights multiply whatever it is given.
//
// The weights make every plausible mix-up land on its own wrong number:
// hand the body the counter as the value (0,1,2,3) and the total is 14;
// hand it the element as the index (10,20,30,40) and it is 200. Only the
// true pairing also prints 200 -- which is why the body prints i beside
// v as well: neither half can be right alone, and any single-slot slip
// lands on its own row of the walk.
//
// THE ELEMENTS ARE TYPED usize EXPLICITLY, not left as a bare literal
// list. A bare `[10, 20, 30, 40]` settles `[int, N]` -> i32, and the
// walk's index is a usize -- so once #777's fix settles the receiver's
// element INTO the bound (`sema_apply.infer_array_bound`), `total + i * v`
// asks the settled-operands question (`sema_operand.operands_agree`,
// issues #725/#731: two settled integer operands are the same one, and
// usize has no lossless conversion to i32 to reach for). Before that fix
// this line was green INSIDE the bug: `v` never settled, so the mixed-
// width pair was never asked. Writing the element type down keeps the
// weights arithmetic legal on its own terms -- and still discriminates,
// because an index pointed at the value slot sums to 14, not 200.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    xs = [usize, 4](10, 20, 30, 40);
    total ::= 0;
    xs.loop((h, i, v) {
        total = total + i * v;
        println("{} {}", i, v);
    });
    println("{}", total);
    Ok(0);
}
